  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nForecast remains complex through mid-week, as a nearly \nstationary frontal boundary lingers across the forecast area \nbeneath an active west-northwest flow pattern aloft. Periodic \nshower and thunderstorm chances will persist with somewhat low \nconfidence in timing and coverage, ultimately modulated by \nindividual short waves and daily diurnal heating and instability\ntrends. Episodes of severe weather and locally heavy rainfall \nremain possible.\n\nIn the near-term, latest surface analysis depicts a nearly \nstationary frontal boundary stretching from an area of low \npressure over western KS northeast into western IA, then east \nalong roughly the I-80 corridor across northern IL/northwest IN.\nA very warm/humid airmass is in place along/south of the \nboundary, characterized by temps in the upper 70/around 80 and \ndew points in the mid-70s at this late night hour. Aloft, \nseasonably fast west-northwest flow was in place across the \nregion, supplying a track for a series of mid-level short wave \ndisturbances of varying amplitude. One such wave is currently \ntracking from IA into northern IL, supporting a southwesterly 35\nkt low-level jet atop the low-level frontal zone into northeast\nIL/northwest IN. Several clusters of strong thunderstorms had \ndeveloped in the enhanced warm/moist advection regime associated\nwith this jet last evening, with a small MCS remaining across \nour southern tier of cwa counties as of 2 AM on the nose of the \njet in the vicinity of the surface frontal boundary. Given \nML/MUCAPE values in the 3000-3500 J/kg range, 35-40 kts of \neffective shear and precipitable water values near 2 inches, \nsevere weather and locally heavy rainfall remain a concern with\nthese storms for the next hour or so, before they eventually \nslide southeast of the area toward/just after daybreak as the \nlow-level jet gradually veers more westerly and weakens with the\npassage of the mid-level wave.\n\nOnce these storms exit the area, there should be a bit of a \nlull in convection in the wake of the departing short wave and \nduring our diurnal instability minimum. Can't completely rule \nout some isolated elevated shower or thunderstorm development \nhowever, due to the presence of the front/baroclinic boundary \nand weaker but still persistent warm/moist advection. Chances \nshould increase again at least a bit toward midday and into this\nafternoon however, as another weak wave or MCV (currently \napproaching the Omaha vicinity) tracks east during a more \nfavorable diurnal time. CAM guidance loosely depicts this, \nthough differences in coverage and timing details are evident. \nAdditional development also appears possible later this evening,\nas the nocturnal low-level jet modestly increases atop the \nfrontal zone. There does appear to be a somewhat consistent \nsignal however, amongst both CAM and global guidance, for an MCS\nto develop over NE/SD later tonight which would approach the \nforecast area around/after daybreak Tuesday. This supports the \nhighest pops (60-70%) during Tuesday, and have limited other \nmentions to just chance (20-40%) later today and tonight. Given \notherwise similar instability/shear and moisture parameters, we \nremain in SPC/WPC slight risks for severe weather and excessive \nrainfall during these periods. \n\nNo significant change in the pattern is evident into mid-week \n(Wednesday, possibly Thursday), though confidence gradually \ndecreases based on impacts of any preceding convective/MCS \noutflow impacts. Global ensembles continue to show a signal for \nour frontal boundary to shift a little farther south Thursday \nnight and Friday, which may decrease our shower/thunderstorm \npotential briefly late in the week, though this signal is weaker\nthan earlier runs and there are indications that the front may \nlift back into the area next weekend. Thus, it appears that our \nseasonably warm/humid and periodically stormy pattern may \npersist for much of the forecast period.\n\nRatzer",
